is on the program mrs howard as always  good good to have you on and of course a  huge amount for us to talk about this  morning nothing least  at not least of course than that at that  trial uh that is being launched today  just tell us a little bit about it  yeah absolutely so uh the uh  oxford trial is looking at um  how um as has been done in the past with  hepatitis  and of course with polio and measles  mumps and  rubella for children you can look at  mixing uh vaccines and see the impact at  four weeks  and at 12 weeks uh it's an important  trial we're supporting it through the  vaccines task force with seven million  pounds um it will report probably  after the summer and of course it will  have no impact on the deployment which  i'm sure your viewers  will want to hear and so if you're  currently  had the fisa biontech vaccine you will  get your fisa biontech vaccine is your  second  dose the boosted dose and of course if  you have the oxford astrozenica vaccine  you'll get the osteoarthritic vaccine  this is more longer term uh keep  keeping us ahead of the uh or at least  in the leadership position i should say  in the world  in helping the whole world because no  one is safe until we're all safe and  uh if we understand and more about how  we can  use vaccines together uh then we should  be in a much stronger position  in terms of vaccinating the united  kingdom but also the rest of the world  and we await the results of the trial uh  then with with great interest i wonder  if we can focus on one of the vaccines  which is  which is currently being deployed at the  fisa vaccine  a number of people have got in touch  because of some concerns over research  that has been produced in a variety of  places it appears in fact we've got a  number of pieces of research some  suggesting  that there isn't sufficient uh  protection given by just one dose  another piece of research this morning  suggesting that by 21 days after the  first dose  you perhaps enjoyed 90 protection can  you just provide a little bit of  reassurance  to those who have only had one dose of  the fisa vaccine  that there is sufficient efficacy in  just that  absolutely um the mhra our regulator  uh one of the best regulators in the  world and of course the joint committee  on vaccination and immunization and our  chief medical  officers of england scotland wales and  northern ireland have looked at this  and looked at the data and actually  after  15 to 21 days  the single first dose of visor gives  a very high protection of course the  second base then gives you that boost  and that longevity as well and that data  has been  examined thoroughly by all those  different  uh the regulator the joint committee and  of course the chief medical officer  and their recommendation of the 12-week  dosing interval is the right one and  it's what we  will deliver in this vaccination  deployment program the largest  vaccination deployment program  in the history of the nhs very quickly  if we can  just a question on the oxford um vaccine  it's been reported today that they are  already  tweaking the vaccine to deal with the  new perhaps  worrying variants that that are  circulating in the uk at the moment  can you confirm that maybe give us an  idea just roughly of when we might have  that updated vaccine  uh coming up to speed yeah absolutely  neil so  it's very unlikely that the current  vaccines won't be  effective on uh the variance whether the  cancer  other variants especially when it comes  to severe illness and hospitalization so  that is  the good news which is why we're  absolutely moving as fast as we can  to get to that mid-february target of  protecting the top four  cohorts that's 15 million people in the  united kingdom  but all manufacturers fisa biontech  moderna of course oxford astrazeneca  and others are looking at how they can  improve their vaccine  to make sure that we are ready for any  variant there are about 4 000 variants  around the world  of cobalt now we are the largest um  genome sequencing industry we're about  50 of the world genome industry  genome sequencing industry and we're  keeping a library of all the variants  that we're ready to be able to respond  uh whether in the autumn or beyond to  any  uh challenge that the virus may present  and produce the next vaccine  so that we can always protect the united  kingdom and of course offer it to the  rest of the world as well  minister can we be be accurate as to  when we might get a date for the the  hotel quarantine scheme  being up and running there was a  suggestion we might get some form of  announcement from the health secretary  today  that appears to have gone away so  the hotel quarantine is one part of our  border policy of course you know that  people now are required to have  pre-departure testing otherwise they'll  be turned away they have to fill in a  passenger locator form  otherwise they'll be turned away and of  course they have to quarantine  for 10 days which is being enforced much  more strictly including  fires being issued by the police and  much greater police presence at ports  and airports people have to  actually say why they are uh traveling  and travel is restricted only to  essential  uh travel but the quarantine  hotel is being operationalized and that  will play a  you know part of this overall strategy  and the secretary of state for health  next week will be setting out  the operational elements of that policy  as other countries have done you know  germany changed their  their border policy in january as did  canada when these new variants were  appearing and of course we did as well  to strengthen it and make it more robust  and we continue to do so hotel  quarantine next week  so so next week but we understand that  the necessity for it to come on  a as soon as possible it's a separate  system of course but dido harding giving  evidence yesterday  suggested that as many as 20 000 people  who should be  self-isolating under test and tracer  are not doing so given that the system  at the moment is  you know self-isolate if you arrive here  from  from overseas but not in a monitored  hotel  how can we be sure that there isn't a  similar level of non-compliance amongst  international travelers  well first of all um we have restricted  international travel  from those countries where we're worried  about the variant whether it's brazil or  portugal or south africa and we  acted immediately grant shaps the social  state of transport  acted immediately on that but if you are  arriving you have to quarantine  from countries where you are allowed to  come to the united kingdom for 10 days  and we are absolutely using you know  passenger locator forms to know exactly  where you are  but i suppose i suppose the point is  though minister if the system was  working there would be no need for us to  have quarantine hotels  well it's an additional safeguard and  it's right because we've seen other  countries  actually implement it and implement it  well and we think it's the right thing  to do  which is why we're adding it to the uh  you know other  measures that we're taking to make sure  that our borders remain  safe and robust as we vaccinate the  nation  and protect obviously the most  vulnerable so it's in addition to that  it's not the whole of the border policy  sure  can we just have a little bit of clarity  what are the tests  that the government will be applying to  decide whether or not we should move  out of lockdown so as the prime minister  set out yesterday at the press  conference  on the 22nd of february when parliament  returns we'll put  uh two parliament our roadmap of first  opening schools on the 8th of march  so if you work to the date i've just  talked to you about neil which is the  mid-february target  of vaccinating the most vulnerable top  four cohorts  in that phase one so phase one goes down  to nine cohorts  and we'll continue after the mid  february to keep going as fast as we can  in vaccinating all nine mid february if  you add another three weeks to that when  the vaccine  protection really does begin to kick in  you're at the first  week of march and so that's when we will  begin to  first of all open schools and then begin  a  gradual reopening of the economy if we  continue to see good data  and is predicated to your question on  really high quality data of  hospitalization rates coming down we  still have 33 000 people in the hospital  and of course death still remains we are  far too high  and it's a grim uh piece of data but  it does feel though even from the press  conference yesterday that the  case numbers as much as anything else is  going to be one of the metrics the key  metrics that we're using  and you're nodding along but there are  those on your own back benches  who feel that the goal posts have  somewhat been moved originally it was  all about protecting the nhs ensuring  that that was not overly burdened case  numbers of course  if you have cupboard 19 it's certainly  by no means certain that you're going to  end up in hospital  it does feel that that is an additional  barrier to the country coming out of  lockdown  well we'll be able to see much greater  evidence we have two  studies being carried out at the moment  by private by public health england  um one is in uh care home residence it's  the vivaldi study where we're  testing every day because they're in  category one who've been vaccinated  and one with uh healthcare frontline  staff because they're in category two  of the people who've been vaccinated  that's the siren  study that will give us the evidence  of both transmission now we're seeing  really good  evidence from oxford that needs to be  peer reviewed still that transmission  rates for the oxford vaccine  are reduced by two-thirds and of course  protection from serious  um uh illness and and death uh  is almost 100 with the oxford vaccine  very high  with visor for the pfizer biontech  vaccine  as well so we can't yet put a figure  though can we on the number of people  within care homes  workers care home residents that have  actually had the first stores we  we talk about visits we talk about  having been offered  can you just give us a figure on how  many people in england have  have actually had the vaccine and care  homestead so  we've visited over 10 000  care homes uh and we have if offered the  vaccine to all  residents that are eligible there's a  very small  number of residents who currently uh are  infected with covert you have to wait 28  days to revisit  our plan is that we have four visits to  care homes so first visit  is the first dose you return for a  second visit  to if people have been you know have to  wait the 28 days to get the  first dose then this the third visit  second dose  and then a fourth visit for actually  sort of trying to catch anyone who  hasn't  been vaccinated we have offered it to  each and every care home  in the uk by last weekend and of course  the care home uh workers the people who  look after the residents as well  that number is rising uh because they  have options to go  to hospital hubs and to their gps  as well as being vaccinated in the care  home and so  that has been a huge success uh in terms  of  uh delivery and we continue to do the  other categories that's category one  then you've got to go through all the  other categories we're at 90  of over 80s 90 of over 75s  and 50 of over 70s already vaccinated

